comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T10:04:53.215Z","harness:opencode","Candidate edge for the changelog-safe batch (found 2026-07-26): pm-cu1i (closed, token-budget ratchet) -> related pm-a22j (closed, token baseline). The ratchet's body states it turns the a22j one-time baseline into a permanent CI ratchet; the edge is semantically true but touches two terminal items, so it belongs to this wave, not an ad-hoc pass."
body: "## Scope, from the live audit\nFour legacy codes on terminal items only: ninety-eight retired no-active-blocker sentinel references; eight duplicated relationship spellings between terminal endpoints; eight terminal-only ordering cycles spanning forty-nine items, the largest across twenty-three closed milestone items; three dangling references to absent targets.\n\n## Why this needs to be a batch and not opportunistic cleanup\nTouching a closed item updates its timestamp, and the changelog buckets by that timestamp — so ad hoc repair silently drags years-old closed work into the current release notes. Doing all of it at once, deliberately, with the changelog effect measured and regenerated as the final step, is the only safe shape.\n\n## Method\nRead-only classification first, producing the full edge list per code. Then a single mutation batch. Then changelog regeneration and an explicit statement of which buckets moved and why. Never mutate a closed item outside this batch.\n\n## Boundary\nRemoval of meaningless edges only. This wave adds no new relationships — enrichment is the separate active and terminal backfill waves."
